Long COVID Cardiopulmonary Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-viral endothelial dysfunction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-023
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute chest tightness and shortness of breath to rule out pulmonary embolism or acute coronary syndrome.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported new-onset, unremitting chest tightness and increased exertional dyspnea in a patient with a post-viral syndrome. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was absolutely mandated to differentiate functional post-viral endothelial pain from life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergencies prior to regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: There is a heavy band crushing my chest, and I can't seem to draw a satisfying, deep breath.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 24-hour exacerbation of known Long COVID symptoms. The patient developed acute chest tightness, a feeling of "air hunger" (shortness of breath), and a persistent dry cough. The chest pain is described as a diffuse, tight band without radiation to the jaw or arm, minimally worsened by deep inspiration. Symptoms were triggered by minor physical exertion yesterday. The patient expresses severe fatigue and anxiety regarding potential heart failure.
Functional Status: Ambulating short distances but strictly resting due to dyspnea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high anxiety compounding the subjective sensation of dyspnea.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Anxious, taking frequent shallow breaths, no accessory muscle use, conversing in full sentences.
Physical Exam: Chest is clear to auscultation bilaterally with good air entry; no wheeze, crackles, or pleural rub. Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rate and rhythm, normal S1/S2, no murmurs. No calf swelling or tenderness. Chest wall is diffusely tender to palpation (costochondritis overlay). Clinical picture aligns with post-viral endothelial dysfunction and microvascular angina rather than macrovascular occlusion.
Environmental Observations: Well-ventilated room; patient seated upright in a chair.
Medication Reconciliation: No current cardiac medications. Takes vitamin supplements.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Long COVID Cardiopulmonary Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintenance of normal oxygen saturation on room air, absence of pleuritic (sharp, stabbing) pain, clear lung fields, and absence of DVT signs.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is reproducible on chest wall palpation, non-radiating, and patient lacks primary cardiovascular risk factors aside from the post-viral state.

Acute Asthmatic Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of expiratory wheeze and lack of prior atopic/asthma history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of acute chest tightness and dyspnea inherently requires exclusion of fatal pathologies. Urgent home assessment allowed for objective measurement of hemodynamics and oxygenation, alongside thorough cardiopulmonary auscultation. By confirming normal saturations, a clear chest, and identifying prominent chest wall hyperalgesia, acute PE and ACS were clinically de-prioritized. This stabilization in the home prevented a highly taxing and stressful ED presentation, while addressing the painful microvascular endothelial flare.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of Celecoxib 200mg daily to manage endothelial and costochondral inflammation. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Tadalafil, COX-2 inhibitors, and Vitamin C. Instructed on paced breathing exercises.
Positive Behavioral Change: By practicing square breathing (inhale for 4, hold for 4, exhale for 4) when you feel air hunger, you will actively lower your respiratory rate, directly reversing the anxiety-driven hyperventilation and rewarding yourself with the sensation of a full breath.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Pulse oximeter recommended for home reassurance.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for baseline ECG, D-Dimer, and troponin to formally complete the cardiopulmonary workup.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation via 000 if chest pain suddenly becomes crushing and radiates to the arm/jaw, if severe shortness of breath occurs at rest, or if coughing up blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While clinical signs point to a post-viral flare, definitive exclusion of micro-clots or subtle cardiac injury requires formal laboratory testing.
Clinical Course: Endothelial inflammation frequently causes atypical chest pain and perceived air hunger. Symptoms should slowly respond to targeted anti-inflammatories and strict pacing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your chest pain feels like an elephant sitting on your chest, or if you find yourself gasping for air while sitting completely still.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has left the lining of your blood vessels temporarily inflamed and rigid, causing the tight sensation; the prescribed medication directly lowers this inflammation to relax the chest wall.
Actionable Steps: Strictly limit physical exertion for the next 48 hours; resting the cardiovascular system prevents secondary spikes in endothelial inflammation, reinforcing a stable, steady recovery of your breathing.

